7. `assignment` event: any actor can append an assignment (no authority check!). A worker could self-assign. Low severity but real.

8. `budget` event: `g["budget_agent_hours"] += delta` — no authority check. Any worker can change budgets. Also negative deltas allowed.

9. `kill`/`revive`: no authority check — any worker can kill any node!

10. `direction_update`: no authority check — any actor can rewrite a group's direction.

11. `resource_request`: no check; fine (requests are legit from anyone).

12. The status "resource_request APPROVED/DENIED" matcher: gated on `"resource_request" in summary` and hardcodes `decision_by = "astra"` — any worker can approve their own resource request by emitting a status event with a summary containing "resource_request <id>: APPROVED"! That's a serious authority bypass: workers can self-approve resource requests, and the code hardcodes decision_by="astra". Note the token regex `resource_request\s+(\S+)` takes the first token; a worker's own summary "resource_request w14-rr-...: APPROVED by me" matches. And `r.get("event_id") == rid_s` — the resource request event_id. So yes: **self-approval bypass, no authority check on the decision summary**. This is a concrete, high-severity finding.

